<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>Lampteys on Tour — Japan 2026</name>
<Folder><name>Kyoto</name>
<Placemark><name>Henn na Hotel Premier Kyoto Station Hachijo Exit Front</name><description>Day SEP 19 (SAT) · Hotel · 15 Higashikujo Higashisanno-cho, Minami-ku, Kyoto 601-8004</description><Point><coordinates>135.7622,34.9838,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kyoto Station atrium &amp; Skyway (京都駅)</name><description>Day SEP 19 (SAT) · explore · 京都駅 · Higashishiokoji-cho, Shimogyo-ku, Kyoto · Hiroshi Hara's 1997 glass canyon — ride the cascade of escalators to the 15th-floor Happy Terrace, then walk the Skyway tube across the atrium roof.</description><Point><coordinates>135.759,34.9852,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kyoto Tower (exterior) (京都タワー)</name><description>Day SEP 19 (SAT) · explore · 京都タワー · 721-1 Higashishiokoji-cho, Shimogyo-ku, Kyoto · The white 1964 tower was built to look like a candle lighting the city (locals argued about it then; they're fond of it now). Tonight, just admire it lit up from the station's north side — save the paid deck for never; better views are free elsewhere.</description><Point><coordinates>135.75943,34.98754,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kyoto Station food floors (The Cube / Porta / 10F Ramen Street)</name><description>Day SEP 19 (SAT) · food · Dozens of proper restaurants inside one building: grilled fish teishoku, tempura, soba, udon, tonkatsu, obanzai sets. Zero navigation risk on a jet-lag night — and watching a Japanese station at dinner hour is cultural fieldwork.</description><Point><coordinates>135.75855,34.98577,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>7-Eleven / Lawson first-night safari</name><description>Day SEP 19 (SAT) · food · The Japanese convenience store deserves its legend — and it's research for the whole trip. Egg sandos, onigiri, seasonal sweets, and the drinks wall.</description><Point><coordinates>135.759,34.9843,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sannenzaka &amp; Ninenzaka (三年坂・二年坂)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · explore · 三年坂・二年坂 · Higashiyama-ku, Kyoto · The two preserved pilgrim slopes below Kiyomizu — wooden merchant houses, sweet shops, ceramics, and the Hokan-ji pagoda rising between rooftops.</description><Point><coordinates>135.780877,34.996366,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ishibe-koji Lane (石塀小路)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · explore · 石塀小路 · Shimokawara-cho, Higashiyama-ku · A stone-walled residential alley of ryotei inns most visitors walk straight past — sixty quiet seconds of the Kyoto everyone imagines.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7797,34.9997,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kiyomizu-dera (清水寺)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · activities · 清水寺 · 1-294 Kiyomizu, Higashiyama-ku, Kyoto · The day's anchor. First-time history and the city view from the great stage — at opening it belongs to worshippers and us; by 10:00 it belongs to everyone in Japan.</description><Point><coordinates>135.78498,34.99485,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kodai-ji (高台寺)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · activities · 高台寺 · 526 Shimokawara-cho, Higashiyama-ku, Kyoto · The calm counterweight to Kiyomizu: raked gravel, a rock garden, tea houses in bamboo, and a love story — built in 1606 by Nene for her husband Toyotomi Hideyoshi, the peasant who united Japan.</description><Point><coordinates>135.78063,34.99943,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yasaka Shrine &amp; Maruyama Park (八坂神社)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · activities · 八坂神社 · 625 Gionmachi Kitagawa, Higashiyama-ku · Kyoto's festival shrine — home of July's giant Gion Matsuri — and the hinge between the temple slopes and Gion. The park behind is where lunch can become a bench and a breather.</description><Point><coordinates>135.77848,35.00363,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>SIZUYA bakery — the 06:15 breakfast run (志津屋)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · food · 志津屋 · The cheap-breakfast doctrine starts here: SIZUYA opens at 07:00 in the station (ASTY Road, your side), but the konbini pastry + SIZUYA round two works even earlier. The 'Carnet' — a ham-and-onion roll Kyoto treats as soul food — is ~¥300 and legendary.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7588,34.9858,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Okutan Kiyomizu (yudofu) (奥丹清水)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · food · 奥丹清水 · Simmered-tofu sets in a garden house near Kodai-ji — the definitive Higashiyama lunch, and one of the few 'famous Kyoto meals' that fits both of you perfectly.</description><Point><coordinates>135.780701,34.997829,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>% Arabica Higashiyama</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · food · The minimalist Kyoto-born roaster that conquered the world — its original tiny shop sits by the Yasaka pagoda. Design pilgrimage + caffeine in one.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7796,34.9985,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Arabica Shokudo (アラビカ食堂)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · food · アラビカ食堂 · 93-29 Hoshinocho, Higashiyama Ward, Kyoto 605-0853 · The % ARABICA coffee people doing food — a spare, wood-and-white room in Higashiyama serving a proper Japanese breakfast. It sits in exactly the district you are walking, so it works as the start of the slopes rather than a detour.</description><Point><coordinates>135.77816,34.9984,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kaikado Café (開化堂カフェ)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · food · 開化堂カフェ · 352 Sumiyoshicho, Kawaramachi-dori Shichijo-agaru, Shimogyo-ku, Kyoto · Run by the family who have hand-made copper tea caddies in Kyoto since 1875, in a converted 1927 tram depot. The objects on your table — the caddy, the tray, the spoon — are the product, made by the sixth generation. For a trip built around craft, this is the version you can sit inside.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7644,34.9899,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hanamikoji &amp; the Shirakawa canal (花見小路・白川)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · explore · 花見小路・白川 · Gion, Higashiyama-ku · Teahouse facades on Hanamikoji, then willows and stone bridges along the Shirakawa stream — the single most atmospheric ten minutes in Kyoto after the lanterns come on.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7754,35.0052,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gion Kappa / around Pontocho</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · food · Dinner strategy tonight is 'good izakaya with a clear menu': separate dishes mean both diets eat happily without negotiation. Pontocho alley across the river is wall-to-wall options with riverside atmosphere.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7708,35.0053,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sanjusangen-do (三十三間堂)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · activities · 三十三間堂 · 657 Sanjusangendo-mawari, Higashiyama-ku · A 120-metre hall holding 1,001 life-size golden Kannon statues in ranked rows — 12th-century mass production in the service of the sacred, and one of the few sights that silences everyone who walks in.</description><Point><coordinates>135.771713,34.987895,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kyoto National Museum (京都国立博物館)</name><description>Day SEP 20 (SUN) · activities · 京都国立博物館 · 527 Chaya-cho, Higashiyama-ku · Normally the obvious rainy-day heavyweight — but our research suggests the galleries are likely CLOSED for exhibition changeover during our whole trip window (the big Genji exhibition opens 6 Oct, the day after we leave).</description><Point><coordinates>135.773,34.9903,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>One raked garden: Daisen-in or Zuiho-in (大仙院 / 瑞峯院)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · activities · 大仙院 / 瑞峯院 · 54-1 Murasakino Daitokuji-cho, Kita-ku, Kyoto · Daisen-in's dry garden (1509) compresses a whole mountain river into gravel and stone in a space the size of a kitchen — the textbook example of the form, with a monk usually on hand to explain it. Zuiho-in, quieter, has a 1960s garden by Shigemori Mirei with waves raked around rocks. Pick one; both</description><Point><coordinates>135.7456,35.0443,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Daitoku-ji lanes + one stone garden (大徳寺)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · activities · 大徳寺 · 53 Murasakino Daitokuji-cho, Kita-ku · The complex's walled lanes are a pleasure in themselves — earthen walls, pines, gravel, almost no one. Walk them between the garden and lunch.</description><Point><coordinates>135.74606,35.04361,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Izusen (Daiji-in garden) — shojin ryori (泉仙 大慈院店)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · food · 泉仙 大慈院店 · Shojin ryori — the monks' plant-based cuisine — served in red lacquer bowls that nest into each other as you finish, in the garden of the very temple we're visiting. The easiest shared meal of the trip: nothing to negotiate.</description><Point><coordinates>135.74482,35.0418,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>SOU・SOU (Nakano-cho alley) (ソウソウ)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · explore · ソウソウ · Nakano-cho, Nakagyo-ku, Kyoto · Nine tiny themed shops in one backstreet block — modern textile design built on traditional patterns, from split-toe tabi shoes to bold furoshiki. This is the Kyoto answer to Mica's design brief: heritage technique, completely contemporary output.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7661,35.0043,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ippodo Tea — Kaboku Tearoom (一保堂茶舗 喫茶室嘉木)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · activities · 一保堂茶舗 喫茶室嘉木 · Teramachi-dori Nijo-agaru, Nakagyo-ku · A tea merchant since 1717 where the tearoom hands YOU the pot: staff coach you through brewing your own gyokuro or matcha. Learning-by-doing beats being served — and it's the day's best sit-down.</description><Point><coordinates>135.767384,35.014436,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Haru Cooking Class — 13:00, the day's one fixed thing (はる料理教室)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · activities · はる料理教室 · Kamigyo-ku area, Kyoto · A home cooking class in a Kyoto house — obanzai (Kyoto home cooking), taught in fluent English, menus customised to diets as a matter of routine. The skill you take home outlasts every souvenir.</description><Point><coordinates>135.774,35.0402,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nishiki Market graze (big-lunch option) (錦市場)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · food · 錦市場 · The set-lunch/market strategy, Kyoto edition: tamagoyaki on a stick, yuba, grilled scallops, pickles, sesame ice cream. Touristy, yes — but before 11:00 it still belongs to the shopkeepers, and it sits five minutes from Ippodo.</description><Point><coordinates>135.76498,35.00499,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion) (金閣寺)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · activities · 金閣寺 · 1 Kinkakuji-cho, Kita-ku, Kyoto · The shogun's retirement villa turned temple, wrapped in gold leaf above its mirror pond. Yes, everyone comes — because it delivers. By bike you arrive on your own schedule, not a bus timetable's.</description><Point><coordinates>135.72925,35.03937,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kyoto Eco Trip bike hire + Kinukake-no-michi ride</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · activities · Near Kyoto Station · City-authorised rental with online booking and an English cycling map. The Kinukake-no-michi ('silk-draped road', named for an emperor who wanted a snowy hill in summer and had one draped in white silk) runs Kinkaku-ji → Ryoan-ji → Ninna-ji in 2.5 km.</description><Point><coordinates>135.75716,34.9831,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kitano Tenmangu evening opening (北野天満宮)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · activities · 北野天満宮 · Bakuro-cho, Kamigyo-ku · Kyoto students still pray here before exams — Sugawara no Michizane, a 9th-century scholar wronged at court, became the god of learning after his death (and some well-timed lightning strikes on his enemies).</description><Point><coordinates>135.7352,35.0313,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Toji Kobo-ichi flea market (東寺 弘法市)</name><description>Day SEP 21 (MON) · explore · 東寺 弘法市 · 1 Kujocho, Minami-ku, Kyoto 601-8473 · A monthly market in the grounds of a ninth-century temple, under Japan's tallest wooden pagoda. Dealers, farmers, junk stalls and serious antiques side by side — the same category of thing as the Tokyo markets you saved, at Kyoto prices and on a date that actually lands.</description><Point><coordinates>135.747786,34.980607,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Higashi Hongan-ji (東本願寺)</name><description>Day SEP 23 (WED) · activities · 東本願寺 · 754 Tokiwa-cho, Karasuma-dori, Shimogyo-ku · One of the world's largest wooden buildings, seven minutes from the hotel, free, and strangely uncrowded. On the day Japan remembers its dead, the head temple of its most popular Buddhist school is the right room to stand in.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7583,34.9913,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shosei-en Garden (渉成園)</name><description>Day SEP 23 (WED) · activities · 渉成園 · Higashitamamizu-cho, Shimogyo-ku · Higashi Hongan-ji's detached stroll garden: ponds, bridges, tea pavilions and almost nobody in it — the quiet equinox alternative if the big hall feels too big.</description><Point><coordinates>135.76318,34.9914,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ohagi — the equinox sweet (おはぎ)</name><description>Day SEP 23 (WED) · food · おはぎ · The whole country eats these today: soft rice wrapped in coarse red-bean paste, made for the higan week when families honour their ancestors. Buying yours from a depachika counter alongside everyone else IS the cultural event.</description><Point><coordinates>135.75855,34.98554,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fushimi Inari Taisha (伏見稲荷大社)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · activities · 伏見稲荷大社 · 68 Fukakusa Yabunouchi-cho, Fushimi-ku · Head shrine of the rice god Inari since 711 — older than Kyoto. Each gate is a donated prayer for prosperity (names and dates painted on the back); fox statues guard everything, holding keys to the rice store in their teeth.</description><Point><coordinates>135.772672,34.967149,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kyoto Station dinner, second lap</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · food · You know the building now. Tempura counter, grilled fish teishoku, or the izakaya floor — an easy cooked dinner with zero navigation tax before an early night.</description><Point><coordinates>135.75855,34.98554,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mo-an (茂庵)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · explore · 茂庵 · 8 Yoshida Kaguraokacho, Sakyo-ku, Kyoto · A wooden teahouse cafe at the top of Mount Yoshida, with big windows over the trees and the city beyond. Left over from a Taisho-era tea-ceremony estate, and reached only on foot through the woods.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7871,35.02633,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kamo delta (鴨川デルタ)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · explore · 鴨川デルタ · Demachiyanagi, Sakyo-ku, Kyoto · Where the Takano and Kamo rivers meet, there are stepping stones shaped like turtles and plovers set across the water, and grass banks where Kyoto students, families and old men with newspapers spend entire afternoons doing nothing. It is the most ordinary and most loved public space in the city.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7715,35.031,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Taishu Sakaba Muni</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · food · A cheap, loud, local standing bar in the Kiyamachi/Pontocho block — the opposite of a considered Kyoto evening, and useful for exactly that reason. Its signature is an absurd balloon-sized sour glass that costs the same as the normal one.</description><Point><coordinates>135.7708,35.0085,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>rokujuan</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · food · Small stone bowls of jelly and mochi sweets — pink with fruit, matcha with black beans, one clear with gold flake, one deep blue topped with an edible pansy. Saved from a set about the parts of Kyoto tourists do not reach.</description><Point><coordinates>135.75533,35.00727,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Osaka</name>
<Placemark><name>Osaka Castle (grounds + main keep) (大阪城)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · activities · 大阪城 · 1-1 Osakajo, Chuo-ku, Osaka · Japan's most consequential castle site: Hideyoshi's golden-age fortress, the 1615 siege that ended his line, and a 1931 concrete reconstruction that is itself now historic. The 8F deck gives the whole city.</description><Point><coordinates>135.5259,34.6873,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Amerikamura (アメリカ村)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · アメリカ村 · Nishishinsaibashi, Chuo-ku · 'Ame-mura' has been Osaka's youth-culture import zone since the 70s — vintage stores, streetwear, record shops, and a small Statue of Liberty on a rooftop. Louder and scrappier than anything in Tokyo's equivalent districts.</description><Point><coordinates>135.4977,34.67195,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Orange Street (Tachibana-dori) (立花通り)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · 立花通り · Minamihorie, Nishi-ku · Four hundred metres of furniture showrooms, plant-led concept stores, ceramics and quiet fashion — the grown-up sibling to Ame-mura, ten minutes' walk west in Horie.</description><Point><coordinates>135.496277,34.671036,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kuromon Ichiba Market (big-lunch graze) (黒門市場)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · food · 黒門市場 · The market-lunch strategy, Osaka edition: grilled scallops and prawns off the flame, tamagoyaki, wagyu skewers for Dad, fruit for the road. Ten minutes from Amerikamura — graze here INSTEAD of a sit-down lunch.</description><Point><coordinates>135.5063,34.6657,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kougaryu / street takoyaki (甲賀流)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · food · 甲賀流 · Osaka's mandatory street food, eaten standing in Amerikamura where every local generation has done the same. Molten inside — the first-timer burn is a rite of passage; warn Dad anyway.</description><Point><coordinates>135.4977,34.6723,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shinsekai &amp; Tsutenkaku (新世界)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · 新世界 · Ebisuhigashi, Naniwa-ku · Built in 1912 half-modelled on Paris, half on Coney Island, then left gloriously unpolished. The billboards, the kushikatsu barkers and the tower make it Osaka's most photogenic time capsule.</description><Point><coordinates>135.506308,34.652508,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hozenji Yokocho &amp; the mossy Fudo (法善寺横丁)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · 法善寺横丁 · 1-2-16 Namba, Chuo-ku · One lantern-lit stone alley behind Dotonbori, and a temple statue so covered in moss it looks knitted — decades of visitors ladling water over Fudo-myoo with their wishes. Make a wish each; the contrast with what's 50 metres away is the whole lesson of Osaka.</description><Point><coordinates>135.5023,34.6675,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dotonbori &amp; the Glico Man (道頓堀)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · 道頓堀 · Dotonbori, Chuo-ku · The canal-side neon gorge: the running Glico Man (since 1935), the giant mechanical crab, and every food mascot in three dimensions. Pure sensory overload — the culture-shock hit you both ordered.</description><Point><coordinates>135.50122,34.66875,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>THE SUMO HALL HIRAKUZA (evening show — calendar check needed) (THE SUMO HALL 平樂座)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · activities · THE SUMO HALL 平樂座 · Namba Parks 8F, 2-10-70 Nambanaka, Naniwa-ku · Namba's 2024-built sumo hall: retired wrestlers, English-language show, bento and — in the premium boxes — an audience member in an inflatable sumo suit challenging a real rikishi. The catch for us: it normally closes TUESDAYS, and 22 Sep is a (holiday) Tuesday.</description><Point><coordinates>135.501998,34.661272,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mizuno (Dotonbori okonomiyaki) (美津の)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · food · 美津の · A Michelin Bib Gourmand okonomiyaki house running since 1945 — about 20 counter seats around the griddle. Seafood-or-vegetable pancakes for Mica, pork-or-mixed for Dad, cooked in front of you: the perfect two-diet dinner.</description><Point><coordinates>135.50323,34.66837,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Namba Ramen Ichiza (なんばラーメン一座)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · food · なんばラーメン一座 · EDION Namba, Namba, Chuo-ku, Osaka · A whole floor of ramen shops dressed as an old Japanese street — paper lanterns, wooden shopfronts, a different regional style at each counter. It solves the Dotonbori problem: proper food, no queue roulette, and you get to walk the lane and compare before committing.</description><Point><coordinates>135.5022,34.6663,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ichiran Dotonbori (一蘭 道頓堀店)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · food · 一蘭 道頓堀店 · 1-4-16 Dotonbori, Chuo-ku, Osaka — the ANNEX (別館), on the Sennichimae side · The famous solo-booth chain — you sit in a partitioned counter stall, fill in a paper form for broth richness, garlic, spice and noodle firmness, and a pair of hands passes the bowl through a bamboo blind. Genuinely odd, genuinely good, and a culture-shock lunch that needs no Japanese at all.</description><Point><coordinates>135.503223,34.668437,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tenjinbashisuji Shopping Street (天神橋筋商店街)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · 天神橋筋商店街 · Tenma / Minamimorimachi, Kita-ku, Osaka · Six numbered blocks (1-chome to 6-chome) running north from Minamimorimachi. The 4th block is the stretch that gets recommended — busy, unglamorous, full of food.</description><Point><coordinates>135.5115,34.7025,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nakazakicho (中崎町)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · 中崎町 · Nakazakicho, Kita-ku, Osaka · A pocket of pre-war wooden nagaya houses that survived the bombing, ten minutes' walk from the Umeda skyscrapers — now converted one by one into vintage shops, tiny galleries and coffee places by artists who moved in from the early 2000s. It is the quiet, low-rise counterweight to Amerikamura, and a</description><Point><coordinates>135.50526,34.70692,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Eat as you walk (Tenjinbashisuji)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · food · The arcade is the meal. Takoyaki stands, old kissaten, standing bars and cheap teishoku counters the whole length.</description><Point><coordinates>135.511822,34.704529,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tower of the Sun (太陽の塔)</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · explore · 太陽の塔 · Expo '70 Commemorative Park, Senribanpakukoen, Suita, Osaka · The surviving centrepiece of Expo '70. Okamoto refused the modernist brief and built something closer to a deity. The interior reopened in 2018 after decades sealed.</description><Point><coordinates>135.53231,34.80964,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Rihga Royal Hotel Osaka — Main Lounge</name><description>Day SEP 22 (TUE) · food · A pink-chandelier grand hotel lounge with floor-to-ceiling windows onto a garden waterfall, serving proper matcha and wagashi. It is the calm, sit-down, air-conditioned counterweight to a day of walking, and it is easy to reach from Osaka Station.</description><Point><coordinates>135.4877,34.6906,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Arashiyama &amp; Sagano</name>
<Placemark><name>HOSHINOYA Kyoto</name><description>Day SEP 23 (WED) · Hotel · 11-2 Arashiyama Genrokuzan-cho, Nishikyo-ku, Kyoto 616-0007</description><Point><coordinates>135.666638,35.016365,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Arashiyama Yusai-Tei Gallery (嵐山 祐斎亭)</name><description>Day SEP 23 (WED) · explore · 嵐山 祐斎亭 · Arashiyama, Ukyo-ku, Kyoto · A historic riverside residence turned dyeing gallery — rooms of flowing dyed silk, and the famous mirror-table room where a lacquered surface reflects the window and the gorge into a single image. Straight down the middle of the design interest on this trip.</description><Point><coordinates>135.67117,35.01469,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The gorge arrival (boat or shuttle) (嵐峡)</name><description>Day SEP 23 (WED) · activities · 嵐峡 · 60 Arashiyama Nakaoshita-cho, Nishikyo-ku · From the lounge by Togetsukyo bridge, the hotel carries you up the wooded gorge to a property with no road frontage. It's a 15-minute drift between cliffs; on the rare day the river says no, the hotel's car keeps the same ritual of leaving the city behind.</description><Point><coordinates>135.669,35.0128,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dinner at the retreat — à la carte, the smart way</name><description>Day SEP 23 (WED) · food · The ¥20,000 kaiseki is skipped by design. The dining hall's à la carte menu (17:30–22:00) can't be booked ahead — you order on the day, which suits us: yuba udon, bubuzuke (tea-poured rice), Kyoto curry, a beef bowl. Dish prices aren't published; guest reports put them around ¥1,500–3,500 each, so b</description><Point><coordinates>135.666638,35.016365,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Morning Stretch on the riverside deck (朝のストレッチ)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · activities · 朝のストレッチ · On property · The hotel's guided outdoor stretch session — slow, yoga-adjacent, done on the deck with the gorge as the studio wall. Free for guests, which makes it the best-value thing the property offers.</description><Point><coordinates>135.669,35.0132,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>September evening: sake to cricket song (虫の音に聞き入る酒)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · activities · 虫の音に聞き入る酒 · On property · Through September the hotel pours complimentary evening sake in the garden while the crickets do the programming — the Japanese art of listening to autumn insects (mushi-kiki), practised since the Heian court, with a drink in hand.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6744,35.01131,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>In-room asa-nabe breakfast (the treat)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · food · The famous HOSHINOYA breakfast, served at your window over the river: a morning hot pot built on Morika tofu and yuba, finished as zosui (savoury rice porridge) in the broth. One morning of this is the treat; the other morning stays simple.</description><Point><coordinates>135.674398,35.011312,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tenryu-ji &amp; the Sogenchi garden (天龍寺)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · activities · 天龍寺 · 68 Susukinobaba-cho, Saga-Tenryuji, Ukyo-ku · A UNESCO Zen temple whose 14th-century garden survives in its original design — the pond borrows Arashiyama's hills as backdrop, the technique (shakkei, 'borrowed scenery') that Japanese gardens are famous for.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6738,35.0159,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bamboo Grove (竹林の小径)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · activities · 竹林の小径 · Saga-Ogurayama, Ukyo-ku · The green corridor every photo promises — and at 07:30 on a post-holiday Thursday, it can still deliver the hush. The sound of wind in this bamboo is on Japan's official '100 soundscapes' list.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6716,35.017,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Okochi Sanso Villa (大河内山荘)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · activities · 大河内山荘 · 8 Tabuchiyama-cho, Saga-Ogurayama, Ukyo-ku · A silent-film star spent thirty years' earnings building this hillside garden estate. Entry includes matcha and a sweet in the tea house — and the crowds never make it this far up the hill.</description><Point><coordinates>135.669363,35.017249,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shigetsu (inside Tenryu-ji) (篩月)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · food · 篩月 · Lunch inside the temple grounds, cooked to the same monastic principles the garden was built on — if Plan B runs long, this is the coherent way to extend it.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6744,35.0156,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Unagiya Hirokawa — the village dinner escape (うなぎ屋 廣川)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · food · うなぎ屋 廣川 · If the à la carte hall doesn't tempt, this is the reason to take the evening car down: Arashiyama's Michelin Bib Gourmand eel house, unagi grilled over charcoal — and one of the only good places here that actually serves dinner (most of Arashiyama shuts by 17:30). The hotel runs an evening car to th</description><Point><coordinates>135.67827,35.01719,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Arashiyama Kijuro — the no-booking backup (嵐山 喜重郎)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · food · 嵐山 喜重郎 · The walk-in fallback if Hirokawa is full: garden-view dining room serving until 19:30, wagyu rice bowls for one side of the table and a yudofu (simmered tofu) set for the other. No reservations taken, which for a fallback is exactly right.</description><Point><coordinates>135.677409,35.016797,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Adashino Nenbutsu-ji (化野念仏寺)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · explore · 化野念仏寺 · 17 Sagatoriimoto Adashinocho, Ukyo-ku, Kyoto · A hillside temple in upper Sagano with its own bamboo grove — two separate accounts recommended it specifically as the answer to the famous grove being unwalkable with crowds. The temple grounds also hold a field of small stone Buddhas.</description><Point><coordinates>135.66452,35.02679,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Saga Toriimoto preserved street (嵯峨鳥居本)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · explore · 嵯峨鳥居本 · Sagatoriimoto, Ukyo-ku, Kyoto · A short preserved street of thatched and machiya houses hung with red-and-white paper lanterns, at the very top of the Arashiyama walk. One creator calls it simply her favourite walk in Kyoto.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6655,35.0298,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Saihō-ji — the Moss Temple (西芳寺)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · explore · 西芳寺 · 56 Matsuo Jingatanicho, Nishikyo-ku, Kyoto · One of the oldest Zen garden forms in Japan — a pond garden under a hundred and twenty species of moss, and the direct ancestor of every dry garden that came after it. Access is capped, so it is one of the few famous Kyoto gardens you can still experience quietly.</description><Point><coordinates>135.683712,34.992181,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gio-ji (祇王寺)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · explore · 祇王寺 · 32 Sagatoriimoto Kozakacho, Ukyo-ku, Kyoto · A tiny thatched hermitage standing in a garden carpeted entirely in moss, three minutes' walk from Adashino Nenbutsu-ji. It is named for Gio, a court dancer cast aside by the warlord Taira no Kiyomori, who retreated here to become a nun — the temple is small because grief is small and private.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6653,35.0238,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sagano Romantic Train (Torokko) (嵯峨野トロッコ列車)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · activities · 嵯峨野トロッコ列車 · Sagatenryuji, Ukyo-ku, Kyoto · An open-sided sightseeing train along the top of the same gorge the boat comes down — 25 minutes each way, ¥880 one way, and you can simply ride it back if you do not want the boat.</description><Point><coordinates>135.67907,35.01876,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nakagawa Hatsumeido (中川発明堂)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · food · 中川発明堂 · A small shop in the Arashiyama walking area building burgers on rice buns instead of bread. The name translates as 'Invention Hall', which tells you the spirit. Cheap, quick, takeaway-friendly, and a genuinely Kyoto thing in a form you can eat walking.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6788,35.016,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Elsewhere</name>
<Placemark><name>Hozugawa River boat ride (保津川下り)</name><description>Day SEP 24 (THU) · activities · 保津川下り · Hozucho, Kameoka, Kyoto · A flat-bottomed wooden boat punted and rowed down the Hozu gorge by three boatmen, ending in Arashiyama. About two hours, almost entirely seated, and the scenery does the work. For a day that is supposed to be restful, this is the version of 'going out' that costs the least effort.</description><Point><coordinates>135.58687,35.01724,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Miyama (美山)</name><description>Day SEP 25 (FRI) · explore · 美山 · A village of thatched farmhouses in the northern Kyoto mountains, roughly between the city and the coast. It comes up repeatedly as the example of 'this is still Kyoto' — the prefecture is mostly mountains and sea, and the temple city is a small corner of it.</description><Point><coordinates>135.6215,35.319,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nara Park &amp; the deer (奈良公園)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · explore · 奈良公園 · Nara Park, Nara · About 1,200 wild sika deer roam the park and the temple approaches. They have been protected since the 8th century as messengers of the Kasuga gods, and they have learned that a bow gets a cracker. Bow to a deer and it bows back — then, if you are holding shika-senbei, all of its friends arrive.</description><Point><coordinates>135.843,34.6851,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Todai-ji — the Great Buddha Hall (Daibutsuden) (東大寺 大仏殿)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · activities · 東大寺 大仏殿 · 406-1 Zoshicho, Nara · A 15-metre bronze Buddha cast in 752, inside a wooden hall so big that the current version (rebuilt 1709) is two-thirds the size of the original and still one of the largest wooden buildings in the world. You come in through the Nandaimon gate past two 8-metre guardian statues carved in 1203. Nothin</description><Point><coordinates>135.83982,34.68903,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nigatsu-do — the terrace over Nara (二月堂)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · activities · 二月堂 · 406-1 Zoshicho, Nara · A wooden hall on stilts up the hillside behind the Great Buddha, with a wraparound veranda that looks west over the whole of Nara to the hills — free, open late, and the place locals bring visitors at sunset. Its March fire festival (Omizutori) has run every year without a break since 752.</description><Point><coordinates>135.8446,34.6921,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kasuga Taisha — the lantern shrine (春日大社)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · activities · 春日大社 · 160 Kasuganocho, Nara · The Fujiwara family's shrine, in a primeval forest that has been off-limits to logging for a thousand years. Three thousand lanterns — stone ones lining the approach, bronze ones hanging in the vermilion corridors — donated over centuries the way Fushimi Inari's gates were.</description><Point><coordinates>135.8484,34.6814,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kofuku-ji — the temple by the station (興福寺)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · activities · 興福寺 · 48 Noboriojicho, Nara · The Fujiwara clan's family temple, two minutes from Kintetsu-Nara, whose five-storey pagoda is Nara's postcard. Its National Treasure Museum holds the six-armed Ashura, the most loved Buddhist statue in Japan.</description><Point><coordinates>135.832,34.683,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nakatanidou — the mochi-pounding shop</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · food · The shop on Sanjo-dori famous for the fastest mochi-pounding in Japan — two men hammering rice dough in a wooden mortar, one turning it between blows, at a speed that has been on every travel show since the 1990s. The mochi is warm, green, soft and ¥200. Perfectly on the way from the park to JR Nara</description><Point><coordinates>135.828,34.6826,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kibune (貴船)</name><description>Day SEP 27 (SUN) · explore · 貴船 · Kurama Kibunecho, Sakyo-ku, Kyoto · A one-street mountain village along a fast stream, with Kifune Shrine at the top and kawadoko dining platforms strung along the water below.</description><Point><coordinates>135.76222,35.12139,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Ine &amp; the north coast</name>
<Placemark><name>Ine Funaya Miyabi / Miyabi Bettei</name><description>Day SEP 25 (FRI) · Hotel · 464 Hide, Ine-cho, Yosa-gun, Kyoto 626-0425</description><Point><coordinates>135.288,35.692,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Amanohashidate View Land (the 'flying dragon' view) (天橋立ビューランド)</name><description>Day SEP 25 (FRI) · activities · 天橋立ビューランド · Monju, Miyazu, Kyoto · Chairlift up, then perform the matanozoki: turn your back, bend, and view the sandbar upside-down between your legs — it 'flies' into the sky. Two grown adults doing this next to strangers doing the same is exactly the trip's brand of memorable.</description><Point><coordinates>135.182118,35.552789,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Station-area teishoku + provisions run</name><description>Day SEP 25 (FRI) · food · Lunch here is strategy: cooked-fish or noodle sets by the station, then the small supermarket/shops for tomorrow's breakfast, water, snacks and emergency food — the last easy shopping before the village.</description><Point><coordinates>135.1875,35.5672,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The funaya waterfront walk (伊根の舟屋)</name><description>Day SEP 25 (FRI) · explore · 伊根の舟屋 · Ine-cho, Yosa-gun, Kyoto · A slow first lap of the bay road: boat garages at water level, nets drying, the sea licking at living rooms. Nothing in Japan looks like this.</description><Point><coordinates>135.28978,35.67481,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mukai Shuzo — the sake tasting (向井酒造)</name><description>Day SEP 25 (FRI) · activities · 向井酒造 · 67 Hirata, Ine-cho · Your sake tasting, moved to arrival day on purpose: taste tonight and there are two clear mornings between the sake and Sunday's early bus out. 'Japan's closest brewery to the sea' has run since 1754 with the tide line at the door; the signature Ine Mankai, brewed from ancient red rice by one of Jap</description><Point><coordinates>135.286396,35.674767,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yuhigaura (夕日ヶ浦)</name><description>Day SEP 25 (FRI) · explore · 夕日ヶ浦 · A Sea of Japan beach further along the same north coast you are heading for, named for its sunsets — there is a driftwood rope swing on the sand that has become its signature. Same coastline as Ine, different bay.</description><Point><coordinates>134.962801,35.663247,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sea taxi with a local fisherman (海上タクシー)</name><description>Day SEP 26 (SAT) · activities · 海上タクシー · Ine-cho waterfront · A small boat, a local skipper, and the close pass between the funaya that the big boat can't make — plus whatever stories the fisherman feels like telling. This is the trip's 'local people' brief, delivered.</description><Point><coordinates>135.292,35.6785,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ine-wan Meguri sightseeing boat (alternative) (伊根湾めぐり遊覧船)</name><description>Day SEP 26 (SAT) · activities · 伊根湾めぐり遊覧船 · 11 Hide, Ine-cho · The bigger, steadier loop of the bay — every 30 minutes from Hide dock, no booking, seagulls and black kites shadowing the stern for crackers.</description><Point><coordinates>135.27733,35.67054,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ine Mankai, the slow second helping (向井酒造)</name><description>Day SEP 26 (SAT) · activities · 向井酒造 · 67 Hirata, Ine-cho · The tasting happened on arrival day — today is for drinking the findings slowly: a glass of the rosé-pink red-rice Ine Mankai at INE CAFE with the bay doing the entertainment, and the brewery's sake-rosé soft serve if the sun is out. Nothing about today needs to be efficient.</description><Point><coordinates>135.286396,35.674767,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Funaya-no-Sato Park viewpoint (道の駅 舟屋の里)</name><description>Day SEP 26 (SAT) · activities · 道の駅 舟屋の里 · Kameshima, Ine-cho · The roadside station on the hill gives the free panorama over the whole bay — the postcard shot of all 230 funaya at once, earned by a 10-minute uphill walk.</description><Point><coordinates>135.29114,35.67444,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Funaya Shokudo (舟屋食堂)</name><description>Day SEP 26 (SAT) · food · 舟屋食堂 · The fishermen's-co-op-adjacent lunch counter above the tourist office: the chef buys the morning catch, the menu is whatever the bay provided. As direct as sea-to-table gets.</description><Point><coordinates>135.288,35.692,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Kiso Valley</name>
<Placemark><name>Hostel Yui-an</name><description>Day SEP 28 (MON) · Hotel · 4828 Yomikaki, Nagiso, Kiso District, Nagano</description><Point><coordinates>137.6108,35.62111,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yui-an social evening (結庵)</name><description>Day SEP 28 (MON) · activities · 結庵 · 4828 Yomikaki, Nagiso · Beams that predate the Shinkansen by 150 years, dinner from the garden, hosts with two centuries of house to explain. Ask about winter, depopulation, tourism, and what they'd show a friend with one spare day — then let tomorrow's plan absorb the answers.</description><Point><coordinates>137.6045,35.5905,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yui-an communal dinner</name><description>Day SEP 28 (MON) · food · The night's centrepiece: whatever the garden and the season provided, cooked by the hosts, eaten with strangers who stop being strangers by the second course.</description><Point><coordinates>137.610798,35.621106,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>MOUNTAinn Nagiso</name><description>Day SEP 29 (TUE) · Hotel · 3465-1 Yomikaki, Nagiso-machi, Kiso-gun, Nagano 399-5301</description><Point><coordinates>137.6087,35.5936,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Magome-juku (馬籠宿)</name><description>Day SEP 29 (TUE) · activities · 馬籠宿 · Magome, Nakatsugawa, Gifu · The hill post town: one steep beautiful street of inns, water wheels and sweet shops climbing to a mountain viewpoint. Novelist Shimazaki Toson grew up here and set 'Before the Dawn' — the great novel of this road — in his family's inn.</description><Point><coordinates>137.56831,35.52684,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The forest trail: pass, waterfalls &amp; tea house (中山道)</name><description>Day SEP 29 (TUE) · activities · 中山道 · Nakasendo, Nagiso · The heart of the day: cedar forest, the Magome pass (the high point — it's downhill after), the O-daki and Me-daki waterfalls, stone paving that has carried five centuries of feet, and bear bells you genuinely ring (there are bears; the bells work; it becomes a percussion section).</description><Point><coordinates>137.58,35.549,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tsumago-juku &amp; the Waki-Honjin Okuya (妻籠宿・脇本陣奥谷)</name><description>Day SEP 29 (TUE) · activities · 妻籠宿・脇本陣奥谷 · Azuma, Nagiso, Kiso District · Japan's first preserved townscape — residents signed a charter in 1971: 'don't sell, don't rent, don't destroy'. The Waki-Honjin Okuya (rebuilt 1877 in then-newly-legal hinoki cypress) is the interior the street only hints at: hearth smoke, light shafts, an emperor's bathroom built for a visit that </description><Point><coordinates>137.5951,35.5753,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Trail &amp; Tsumago lunch strategy</name><description>Day SEP 29 (TUE) · food · Rural kitchens close early and sell out — so lunch is layered: trail snacks from Magome, then soba or gohei-mochi in Tsumago before 14:00. The valley's buckwheat and chestnut traditions ARE the regional cuisine.</description><Point><coordinates>137.5953,35.5766,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>MOUNTAinn Nagiso (night two)</name><description>Day SEP 30 (WED) · Hotel · 3465-1 Yomikaki, Nagiso-machi, Kiso-gun, Nagano 399-5301</description><Point><coordinates>137.607975,35.598611,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nezame-no-toko (upper viewpoint) (寝覚の床)</name><description>Day SEP 30 (WED) · activities · 寝覚の床 · Agematsu, Kiso District, Nagano · Square-cut granite blocks stacked in a jade river — viewed from Rinsenji temple's garden terrace (¥200) rather than the steep 20-minute scramble to the riverbed. The whole gorge in fifteen minutes, no knees harmed.</description><Point><coordinates>137.6903,35.7461,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Narai-juku main street (奈良井宿)</name><description>Day SEP 30 (WED) · explore · 奈良井宿 · Narai, Shiojiri, Nagano · A full kilometre of preserved wooden townscape — longest of any post town — with water basins still running, workshops open, and far fewer visitors than Magome. This is where yesterday's walk gets its context: this is what all those stations on the road looked like at full wealth.</description><Point><coordinates>137.81139,35.96528,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kiso-Hirasawa lacquer village (木曽平沢)</name><description>Day SEP 30 (WED) · explore · 木曽平沢 · Kiso-Hirasawa, Shiojiri, Nagano · One stop down the line from Narai and almost nobody gets off. A whole village of working urushi lacquer workshops, protected as a national preservation district — not a museum street but studios where the lacquer is actually applied, layer over layer, in rooms that have done it for four hundred year</description><Point><coordinates>137.83121,35.97864,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kiso-Fukushima checkpoint (alternative town) (福島関所)</name><description>Day SEP 30 (WED) · activities · 福島関所 · Kiso-Fukushima, Kiso District · One of Edo Japan's four great checkpoints, which policed the famous rule 'incoming guns and outgoing women' — the shogunate's border control on its own people. Reconstructed post + museum, plus the free Ue-no-dan old quarter on the walk back.</description><Point><coordinates>137.70255,35.8507,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Narai soba lunch (Echigoya / Matsuya area)</name><description>Day SEP 30 (WED) · food · Buckwheat is the valley's grain and Narai's soba houses serve it in Edo-period rooms. Order zaru (cold) to taste what the altitude does to it.</description><Point><coordinates>137.8175,35.9425,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Tokyo</name>
<Placemark><name>U Place Shimokitazawa by Tranova</name><description>Day OCT 1 (THU) · Hotel · Shimokitazawa, Setagaya-ku, Tokyo 155-0031 — exact street address arrives with the Booking.com check-in details</description><Point><coordinates>139.665922,35.662922,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shimokitazawa vintage lanes (下北沢)</name><description>Day OCT 1 (THU) · explore · 下北沢 · Kitazawa, Setagaya-ku · Tokyo's densest second-hand district: 100+ vintage shops in walkable lanes, plus the theatres and live houses that made the area's name. Today is the scouting lap; Sunday is the buying lap.</description><Point><coordinates>139.668,35.6613,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>SHELTER — a gig every night you're here (下北沢シェルター)</name><description>Day OCT 1 (THU) · activities · 下北沢シェルター · 2-6-10 Kitazawa, Setagaya-ku · Shimokita's legendary 200-capacity basement turns 35 in October 2026 and is marking it with a one-man show EVERY night of the month — so a real Tokyo gig is guaranteed on any evening of your stay, two minutes from the station. Announced so far in your window: LINK (3 Oct, 12:30) and VENUS PETER (3 O</description><Point><coordinates>139.66946,35.66154,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The listening bars — music without a ticket (音楽喫茶)</name><description>Day OCT 1 (THU) · activities · 音楽喫茶 · Shimokitazawa lanes · Shimokita's other music tradition: rooms where the record collection is the entertainment. Jazz Kissa Masako, three minutes from the flat, has poured coffee and jazz since 1953 (closed Thu); tonlist in the Suzunari alley plays jazz on vintage hi-fi (closed Mon–Tue, open your Fri–Sun); CITY COUNTRY C</description><Point><coordinates>139.668077,35.662577,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ponipirica — night-one soup curry (ポニピリカ)</name><description>Day OCT 1 (THU) · food · ポニピリカ · Shimokita's best-loved soup-curry room and the correct first dinner: hot, restorative, five minutes from bed. The house trick is a broth built from roughly 700 shrimp — order it as the base and the bowl turns properly seafood.</description><Point><coordinates>139.668,35.6614,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Shimokita curry hit-list (whole stay)</name><description>Day OCT 1 (THU) · food · You live in Tokyo's curry quarter for four nights — one bowl a day is practically civic duty. The shortlist, all within ten minutes: Spice Kitchen Moona (South-Indian SEAFOOD curry and natural wine — Mica's best fit in the whole town; closed Mon), Curry Spice Gelateria KALPASI (cult three-curry plat</description><Point><coordinates>139.666603,35.660922,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Prada Aoyama &amp; the flagship crawl (プラダ青山)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · explore · プラダ青山 · 5-2-6 Minami-Aoyama, Minato-ku · Herzog &amp; de Meuron's 2003 diamond-quilted glass tower — still the district's masterpiece. Around it within 10 minutes: Ito's Tod's (concrete tree branches), Kuma's Sunny Hills (wooden basket), SANAA's Dior (frosted veils). Enter any of them; browsing is free and staff are used to architecture pilgri</description><Point><coordinates>139.7126,35.6646,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Meiji Shrine at dawn (optional pre-breakfast add-on) (明治神宮)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · activities · 明治神宮 · 1-1 Yoyogikamizonocho, Shibuya-ku · 100,000 donated trees grown into a genuine forest in the middle of Tokyo, and gates that open at sunrise (05:40 in early Oct). At dawn it's joggers, priests, and the sound the city is supposed to make impossible. The gates shut 16:40 in October — dawn is the only uncrowded version.</description><Point><coordinates>139.6993,35.6764,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>THE OMAKASE LUNCH — Hantei, Nezu (CHOSEN)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · food · Friday lunch is the trip's omakase counter, chosen: Hantei, the kushiage house in a gorgeous century-old wooden building one minute from Nezu station. Rounds of panko-fried skewers keep arriving until you say stop — the omakase rhythm with every course cooked, and it's a genuinely easy detour: the C</description><Point><coordinates>139.766144,35.717343,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cat Street (キャットストリート)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · explore · キャットストリート · Jingumae, Shibuya-ku · A pedestrian lane over the culverted Shibuya river linking Harajuku to Shibuya — streetwear flagships, vintage dealers and cafés at human scale, without Takeshita's crush.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7057,35.6676,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Japan Folk Crafts Museum (Mingeikan) (日本民藝館)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · explore · 日本民藝館 · 4-3-33 Komaba, Meguro-ku, Tokyo · Yanagi Soetsu's own house and collection, in a building he designed in 1936 — the founding museum of mingei, the movement that argued the anonymous handmade objects of ordinary life were the equal of any art. Ceramics, textiles, ironwork, baskets, all shown as objects rather than exhibits.</description><Point><coordinates>139.679286,35.660813,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shibuya PARCO — 6F culture floor (渋谷パルコ)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · activities · 渋谷パルコ · 15-1 Udagawa-cho, Shibuya-ku · Nintendo TOKYO, Pokémon Center, Capcom and Jump Shop share the 6th floor — Japanese pop-culture retail at flagship intensity. The 6F opens at 10:00, an hour before the rest; on a Friday afternoon expect queues but not chaos.</description><Point><coordinates>139.6983,35.6624,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shibuya Sky (sunset slot) (渋谷スカイ)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · activities · 渋谷スカイ · 2-24-12 Shibuya, Scramble Square · The open rooftop above Shibuya Scramble Square: 360°, Fuji silhouette on clear evenings, and the crossing boiling below. Sunset (~17:20 on 2 Oct) into night is the slot worth planning a fortnight around.</description><Point><coordinates>139.70212,35.65803,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>d47 Shokudo (Shibuya Hikarie 8F)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · food · A design-project restaurant serving teishoku from all 47 prefectures, with the menu doubling as a design exhibition — and windows over the rail junction. The most Mica-coded dinner in Shibuya, and the calmest.</description><Point><coordinates>139.70345,35.65905,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nonbei Yokocho — Drunkard's Alley (のんべい横丁)</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · food · のんべい横丁 · The hole-in-the-wall dinner doctrine, three minutes from the Sky: two post-war alleys along the tracks, each bar seating 4–8, otoshi charge as your seat rent, strangers becoming neighbours by round two. THIS is the local-dinner Tokyo you asked for.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7016,35.6597,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tokiwatei Shibuya</name><description>Day OCT 2 (FRI) · food · Grill-at-the-table yakiniku with a gimmick worth having: a lemon sour tap at every seat, so the drink is poured by you, at the table, with nothing between wanting one and having one. They call it the zero-second lemon sour.</description><Point><coordinates>139.699341,35.658157,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tsukiji Outer Market — if you start early (築地場外市場)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · explore · 築地場外市場 · Tsukiji 4-chome, Chuo-ku, Tokyo · The street market that outlived the fish market itself: tamagoyaki cooked in front of you, knife shops, dried goods, sea urchin eaten standing up. It appeared in the four-day Tokyo plan you saved, and it fits the cheap-standing-food thread better than almost anything else in the city.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7695,35.6655,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tokyo National Museum (Honkan route) (東京国立博物館)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · 東京国立博物館 · 13-9 Ueno Park, Taito-ku · Japan's oldest museum: national treasures on rotation, the sword rooms, samurai armour, Buddhist sculpture, tea wares and ukiyo-e. The regular ticket covers every building — but the Honkan alone, done slowly, is the right ambition.</description><Point><coordinates>139.77647,35.71876,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ameyoko Market (big-lunch graze) (アメ横)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · food · アメ横 · The market-lunch strategy between museum and Yanaka: 400 metres of stalls from Ueno to Okachimachi — kebabs, fresh-cut fruit, takoyaki, seafood bowls, and standing izakaya that fill with locals from mid-afternoon. Post-war black-market bones, present-day appetite.</description><Point><coordinates>139.775443,35.709038,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Karashibi Miso Ramen Kikanbo — Kanda Honten</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · food · Called the best ramen of a three-week Japan trip by the person who saved it. 'Karashibi' is kara (chilli heat) plus shibi (the tingling numbness of sansho pepper) — two different sensations stacked in one bowl, and you choose how much of each. Kanda is a straight run down from Ueno.</description><Point><coordinates>139.77237,35.69367,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mimasu — the 80-year-old chef's counter</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · food · 4-28-2 Yotsuya, Shinjuku-ku, Tokyo 160-0004 · A twelve-seat family sushi counter in Yotsuya run by a chef in his eighties, with his son now working alongside him. The traveller who saved it called it the cheapest omakase of her trip and better than the hundred-pound counters — and said it felt less like a restaurant than eating in someone's hou</description><Point><coordinates>139.7196,35.6878,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yanaka Ginza &amp; the sunset stairs (谷中銀座)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · explore · 谷中銀座 · 3-13 Yanaka, Taito-ku · A 170-metre shotengai of family shops — croquettes, senbei, tea, cats real and ceramic — approached down the 'Yuyake Dandan' (sunset stairs). Everyday Tokyo retail as it was before konbini.</description><Point><coordinates>139.765635,35.72771,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nezu Shrine (根津神社)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · explore · 根津神社 · 1-28-9 Nezu, Bunkyo-ku · One of Tokyo's oldest shrines (1705 buildings intact — a rarity here) with its own miniature tunnel of vermilion torii. Fushimi Inari's pocket edition, usually near-empty.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7601,35.7203,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kagurazaka (神楽坂)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · explore · 神楽坂 · Kagurazaka, Shinjuku-ku, Tokyo · Stone-paved back alleys climbing a hill, left over from a geisha district, now holding an unlikely density of small French bistros alongside the ryotei. Tokyo's quietest good-looking neighbourhood, and almost nobody puts it on a first-trip list.</description><Point><coordinates>139.73972,35.70157,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kayaba Coffee (kissaten since 1938) (カヤバ珈琲)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · カヤバ珈琲 · 6-1-29 Yanaka, Taito-ku · A corner coffee house running since 1938 on the Yanaka edge — tatami room upstairs, egg sandwiches, and the Showa-era kissaten ritual intact. The sit-down the afternoon is built around.</description><Point><coordinates>139.770721,35.721158,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hantei — the kushiage omakase lunch (はん亭 根津)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · food · はん亭 根津 · 2-12-15 Nezu, Bunkyo-ku · The trip's omakase, and the reason it isn't sushi: rounds of freshly fried skewers arrive six at a time until you say stop, in a three-storey wooden house from 1917 that survived both the earthquake and the war — one minute from Nezu station, which is already on today's route. Moved here from the 2n</description><Point><coordinates>139.76563,35.72018,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Asakusa Sumo Show + chanko lunch (相撲ショー)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · 相撲ショー · Asakusa, Taito-ku · Your sumo, delivered: retired rikishi demonstrate real bouts with English commentary, you eat chanko-nabe (the wrestlers' stew), and audience volunteers challenge a wrestler — inflatable sumo suit provided. Dad versus a former professional is the photograph this trip has been building toward.</description><Point><coordinates>139.79313,35.7142,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Samurai Ninja Museum (basic entry — optional) (サムライ忍者ミュージアム)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · サムライ忍者ミュージアム · 1-8-13 Nishi-Asakusa, Taito-ku · The verified samurai museum (the famous Shinjuku one closed in 2022): armour try-on and guided Sengoku history tours every 15 minutes. Basic entry only — the sword-cutting tier is dropped from the plan.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7928,35.7133,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Senso-ji + Kappabashi kitchen street (浅草寺・かっぱ橋)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · 浅草寺・かっぱ橋 · Nishi-Asakusa, Taito-ku · Tokyo's oldest temple (628 AD founding legend: two fishermen netted a golden Kannon) plus the street where Japan's restaurants buy everything — knives, lanterns, and the plastic food models that deserve a museum of their own.</description><Point><coordinates>139.78906,35.715613,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Honjo Life Safety Learning Center</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · Yokokawa, Sumida City, Tokyo · A disaster-preparedness centre in Sumida where you go through simulators: a magnitude 7 earthquake, torrential rain, and winds strong enough to need a handrail. Free, strange, and genuinely instructive in a country you are visiting during typhoon season.</description><Point><coordinates>139.8152,35.70395,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Monster Party (モンスターパーティー)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · モンスターパーティー · 6-16-3 Soto-Kanda, Chiyoda-ku, Tokyo — Kokusai 6163 Bldg 401 · A tiny retro American diner in Akihabara staffed entirely by people in full one-eyed monster masks, so no human face is ever visible. It bills itself as a human-free cafe. This is the place from the 'ten bizarre places' reel you saved, and it is genuinely as odd as it sounds.</description><Point><coordinates>139.770923,35.704452,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Champion, Golden Gai — sing WITH the locals (ゴールデン街 チャンピオン)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · ゴールデン街 チャンピオン · 1-1 Kabukicho, Shinjuku-ku · Exactly what you asked for instead of a private box: Golden Gai's legendary open-floor karaoke bar — no cover, every drink ¥500, songs ¥100, and the whole room (locals, barflies, lost tourists, now you) as the audience. Dad's song choice will be discussed in this family for years.</description><Point><coordinates>139.70457,35.69373,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Albatross G + the Golden Gai crawl (アルバトロス)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · アルバトロス · 1-1-7 Kabukicho, Shinjuku-ku · The 'crazy trippy bars' clause: Albatross G stacks chandeliers, gilt frames and taxidermy kitsch across three doll-house floors (19:00–05:00 daily, ¥500 cover, drinks ~¥700–1,000). Around it, six alleys and 200 five-seat bars — pick doors with posted prices, pay the small covers as rent, talk to who</description><Point><coordinates>139.7046,35.6939,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Rocky Top — the other kind of singing night (銀座ロッキートップ)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · activities · 銀座ロッキートップ · 7-8-19 Ginza, Chuo-ku, Tokyo — Tokyo Building 3F · A bluegrass and country live house that has been running in Ginza since 1980 — banjo, guitar and a house band playing to a room of about fifty, most nights of the week. This is the bar from the reel you saved captioned 'last thing I expected in Tokyo', and it took some finding: the building is liter</description><Point><coordinates>139.762206,35.66907,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Omoide Yokocho — the yakitori lanes (思い出横丁)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · food · 思い出横丁 · Dinner, hole-in-the-wall edition: two lantern-lit lanes by Shinjuku station's west exit, unchanged since the 1940s — counter seats, charcoal smoke, strangers' elbows. Hop two or three shops instead of booking one restaurant; it's cheaper, better and unrepeatable at home.</description><Point><coordinates>139.6997,35.6939,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jujo Ginza Shopping Street (十条銀座商店街)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · explore · 十条銀座商店街 · 1-4-8 Jujonakahara, Kita City, Tokyo 114-0031 · One of Tokyo's oldest covered shopping streets. Two unrelated accounts — one of them a Tokyo local — independently said the same thing: don't spend your money in Asakusa, come here instead. You can eat a full meal along it for under ¥1,000.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7217,35.76274,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Akabane Ichibangai (赤羽一番街)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · explore · 赤羽一番街 · Akabane, Kita City, Tokyo · Two stops on from Jujo, and the drinking half of the same idea — a lantern-lit lane of izakayas and tiny bars where a Tokyo night out is warm, cheap and completely unpretentious. Oden lanterns, standing bars, no English menus and no one performing for anybody.</description><Point><coordinates>139.720874,35.779286,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Koenji (高円寺)</name><description>Day OCT 3 (SAT) · explore · 高円寺 · Koenji, Suginami-ku, Tokyo · Two stops from Shinjuku on the Chuo line and the actual centre of Tokyo's independent music scene — a dense grid of secondhand clothing shops, record stores, standing bars and small live venues. Scruffy, cheap and entirely unbothered by visitors.</description><Point><coordinates>139.6497,35.7053,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>teamLab Borderless (チームラボボーダレス)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · activities · チームラボボーダレス · Azabudai Hills Garden Plaza B, Minato-ku · Fifty-plus borderless works: waterfalls of light that part around you, lantern fields, crows of fire chasing through rooms. You WILL get lost; that's the design. The 'Bubble Universe' and 'Athletics Forest' rooms are the finale's finale.</description><Point><coordinates>139.74,35.66,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Avatar Robot Cafe DAWN ver.β (分身ロボットカフェ DAWN ver.β)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · activities · 分身ロボットカフェ DAWN ver.β · 3-8-3 Nihonbashi-Honcho, Chuo-ku · The OriHime robots at your table are avatars — their pilots chat, joke, and take your order from wherever they live. The 'Diner' seating (min 2 people) gets a dedicated pilot conversation over a full meal; the café tier is the lighter version.</description><Point><coordinates>139.776156,35.689329,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sunday markets — the fourth option</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · explore · Today is the trip's only Tokyo Sunday, and two antique markets run on Sundays only. Both sell the same category of thing — old ceramics, lacquer, tansu chests, wooden toys, kitchen bits — at a fraction of shop prices, and both are outdoors and free to walk.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7994,35.6716,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>SMALL WORLDS — be turned into a figurine</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · explore · Ariake, Koto City, Tokyo · A vast miniature museum in Ariake where, alongside the model cities, you can be 3D-scanned and made into a tiny painted figurine of yourself — then placed into a model Tokyo street. Around ¥6,600 depending on size.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7883,35.6381,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nakano Broadway (中野ブロードウェイ)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · activities · 中野ブロードウェイ · 5-52-15 Nakano, Nakano-ku · A 1966 shopping complex colonised floor-by-floor by Mandarake and collectors: vintage toys, cels, watches, manga, things with no category. Retro-futurism in its natural habitat — the culture-shock shopping stop.</description><Point><coordinates>139.66555,35.70855,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gotokuji cat temple + Todoroki Valley (Setagaya side-quest) (豪徳寺・等々力渓谷)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · activities · 豪徳寺・等々力渓谷 · 2-24-7 Gotokuji, Setagaya-ku · The birthplace of the beckoning cat: thousands of maneki-neko ranked at Gotokuji (a cat allegedly waved a feudal lord out of a lightning storm here). Pair with Todoroki Valley — a shaded ravine walk REOPENED March 2026 after long closure — and, for the committed, the ASAKO IWAYANAGI parfait nearby (</description><Point><coordinates>139.6478,35.6462,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shimokitazawa: the buying lap (下北沢)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · shopping · 下北沢 · 3-26-4 Kitazawa, Setagaya-ku · Thursday was scouting; today is commitment. New York Joe (in the old bathhouse — first Sunday of the month is 50%-off day, which is TODAY, 4 Oct: chaos, but profitable chaos), Flamingo, Chicago, and the record shops.</description><Point><coordinates>139.6678,35.66,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Uoshin (魚しん), Sangenjaya (魚しん)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · food · 魚しん · 3-14-4 Taishido, Setagaya, Tokyo · If the Setagaya option wins, this is where it should end. Sushi from ¥88 a piece in a scruffy local izakaya where two people ate well for ten to fifteen pounds each. It is the direct rebuttal to paying £40 a head in Ginza, and Sangenjaya is a few minutes from Shimokitazawa on the same side of town.</description><Point><coordinates>139.669313,35.647377,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ebisu Dagashi Bar</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · activities · 1-13-7 Ebisu-Nishi, Shibuya City, Tokyo · A retro izakaya stacked with more than a hundred kinds of dagashi — the cheap penny sweets and snacks that Japanese adults grew up on — where a ¥600 cover plus one drink buys you two hours of helping yourself. It is silly, it is cheap, and it is the sort of thing that makes a better last-night story</description><Point><coordinates>139.7077,35.64757,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Godzilla head, Kabukicho</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · activities · Kabukicho, Shinjuku, Tokyo · A full-size Godzilla head mounted on the Hotel Gracery above the Toho cinema in Kabukicho, visible from the street. Thirty seconds of looking up, no ticket, and a decent last photograph.</description><Point><coordinates>139.70031,35.69528,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Muscle Girls Bar (マッスルガールズバー)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · activities · マッスルガールズバー · 2-41-2 Ikebukuro, Toshima-ku, Tokyo — Hayama Bldg B1F · A basement bar in Ikebukuro staffed by competitive bodybuilders who squat 110kg on a small stage, feed you protein shakes through a syringe, and run a chest-press challenge you can win money on. It issues its own currency, printed Muscle Dollars, which is the fake banknote from the reel you saved.</description><Point><coordinates>139.70978,35.73319,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Last-night curry, properly local</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · food · The farewell dinner is the neighbourhood's own dish: Spice Kitchen Moona's South-Indian seafood curry with a glass of natural wine (the best meat-free curry in the town, open Sundays), or KALPASI's three-curry plate with spice gelato after (also open Sundays). Both are ten minutes from bed and neith</description><Point><coordinates>139.668,35.6614,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tokyo Mentsu-dan (東京麺通団)</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · food · 東京麺通団 · You take a tray, choose your noodles, add your own toppings from the counter, and pay at the end — a canteen format that needs no Japanese and no confidence. Bowls come in under ¥1,000 and it stays open to 23:30.</description><Point><coordinates>139.698351,35.694788,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tofu Cuisine Sorano (豆腐料理 空野) — two branches</name><description>Day OCT 4 (SUN) · food · A wood-lined restaurant built entirely around tofu — set meals with thick grilled and fried tofu as the centre rather than the side. Saved from a plant-based account, which makes it a strong bet on the flexible-eating front.</description><Point><coordinates>139.699,35.6565,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edo Koji street (airside, T3 4F) (江戸小路)</name><description>Day OCT 5 (MON) · explore · 江戸小路 · 2-6-5 Hanedakuko, Ota-ku · A recreated Edo-period street of wooden shopfronts and lanterns inside the departures floor — Japan's farewell gift of one more beautiful, slightly absurd space after security.</description><Point><coordinates>139.7673,35.54596,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Last breakfast, Shimokita</name><description>Day OCT 5 (MON) · food · The closing ritual: whichever bakery or kissaten became 'ours' this week, one final time, unhurried.</description><Point><coordinates>139.66822,35.66138,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
</Document></kml>